From c80759ace0c367137cbf2a1a9ab74fc3b77174f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 20:17:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sdk_fman: fix IRQ handling in FMan sysfs show handlers

show_fm_risc_load() ran its FM_CtrlMonStart()/msleep(1000)/FM_CtrlMonStop()
measurement window under local_irq_save(): sleeping with hardirqs disabled is
illegal, and __schedule() re-enables IRQs on the context switch, so the guard
protected nothing while stalling a world-readable sysfs read for a full second.
Drop it (there is no shared FMan-wrapper lock to serialise concurrent readers;
that pre-existing race is unchanged).

More broadly, the show handlers used local_irq_save() as a pseudo-lock with no
cross-CPU effect. Drop it where it guards nothing:
  - handlers that only read idempotent MMIO / format into the buffer
    (dma_stats, stats, muram_free_sz, ctrl_code_ver, pcd_stats, regs,
     kg_regs, fpm_regs, prs_regs, plcr_regs);
  - indirect KG/policer dumps (cls_plan, profiles, schemes, kg_pe_regs) whose
    dump helper already takes the KG/policer HW spinlock internally.

Keep it only on the three handlers whose helper does an unprotected indirect
register-select (fm_dump_ccqueue -> dma_cam_queue/dma_cmd_queue via fmdmccqdr;
fm_dump_tnum_dbg via fmfp_dra): there local_irq_save() is a weak local-CPU
guard, documented as pending a proper FM-level lock. For those kept sites and
the fm_port handlers, validity checks stay hoisted above local_irq_save() so an
early return cannot leak hardirqs disabled.

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
